<p>German Chancellor Friedrich Merz said he would continue working with President Trump and denied that a planned U.S. withdrawal of about 5,000 troops from Germany was tied to their dispute over the Iran war. The Pentagon announced the drawdown on Friday, with the reduction expected over the next six to 12 months. Merz had questioned whether Washington had an exit plan for the Middle East and criticized its handling of negotiations involving Iran; Trump later called Merz an “ineffectual” leader and said the United States could make deeper cuts. Merz told ARD that Americans remained important partners for Germany inside NATO and that a workable partnership had to allow for differing views.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7mnD8sfL7PEJUso2QkpiroDaVhCaE_YTX6gEGd2cplRSk_eZ3BOsos97wqHXbQsylO1bza-_A_NiZSSzrPLnl0oHy0oxGbekjXiMV9SiyJKdpHmjaMZ3hxuYswCTm5Om9I-JXiq7BaqthM_BrxZcwFESgF2-_Q2owp-e4tzfpeJNUkCedRyb0vnMo4DSrNXVGVlSKbL-RgI1CCO8vQaz9_pNoBdBxmUcwk6beGzUdVKhk1VEnp1SO5YnUX-rtX9W2mi2r9FUXNg' alt='US President Donald Trump and German Chancellor Friedrich Merz reach to shake hands as they meet in the Oval Office at the White House in Washington, DC, March 3, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Iran objective: Merz said he shared Trump’s goal of ensuring Iran never obtains a nuclear weapon, despite criticizing Washington’s handling of the war.</li><li>Wider footprint: Semafor reported Trump was also weighing troop reductions in Italy and Spain, raising the possibility of a broader European pullback.</li><li>Diplomatic calendar: The top U.S. diplomat was due in Rome this week, Semafor reported, citing an Italian outlet.</li></ul><blockquote>There is no connection. - German Chancellor Friedrich Merz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Friedrich Merz: He defended his criticism of Washington’s Iran strategy and said allies should be consulted before military decisions affecting them. (<a href='https://www.lasexta.com/noticias/internacional/merz-quita-hierro-re